---
import Footer from "../../components/shared/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../../components/shared/Header.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/shared/PageHeader.astro";
import ContactBanner from "../../components/shared/ContactBanner.astro";
import LeadBlock from "../../components/shared/LeadBlock.astro";
import FloorPlanDiagram from "../../components/case-studies/FloorPlanDiagram.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Yembo Case Study: Engineering for an AI Platform | Novage"
  canonical="case-studies/yembo"
  description="Seven years as engineering partner to Yembo, an AI startup for moving and insurance in 40+ countries: full-stack development, 3D, video calls and AI."
>
  <Header />
  <div id="content">
    <article>
      <PageHeader
        eyebrow="Case study"
        title="Yembo"
        accent="Long-term engineering partner for an AI platform"
      >
        <p class="lead">
          <a href="https://www.yembo.ai/">Yembo</a> is a growth-stage startup
          building AI for the moving and insurance industries, deployed in{" "}
          <strong>40+ countries</strong> and processing{" "}
          <strong>millions of videos</strong>. For <strong>seven years</strong>,
          our <strong>10+ person team</strong> has worked alongside Yembo's core
          team as its engineering partner — across web, backend, mobile and QA.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a
            class="button primary"
            href="#contact"
            data-umami-event="yembo-discuss"
          >
            Discuss your project
          </a>
        </div>
      </PageHeader>

      <div class="page container content">
        <ul class="key-facts">
          <li>
            <strong>40+</strong> countries using the platform
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Millions</strong> of videos processed
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>7 years</strong> of engineering partnership
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>10+</strong> people across web, backend, mobile and QA
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>About Yembo</h2>
        <p>
          Yembo is a venture-backed startup building what it calls "spatial
          intelligence for every property." It started in the moving industry,
          where its AI understands images and videos of a home for virtual
          surveys, and has grown into insurance, with AI-powered 3D home
          inspections for claims and underwriting, and into property management.
          Yembo holds 10+ granted AI patents, and its platform is deployed in
          more than 40 countries.
        </p>

        <h2>The engagement</h2>
        <p>
          We are not a vendor working through a ticket queue. Our team takes
          responsibility for applications and modules across Yembo's products
          and builds new features together with Yembo's core team — from the
          first design discussions to production.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Full-stack development</strong> — frontend, backend and
            mobile applications
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Automated QA</strong> — end-to-end test automation for the
            products we work on
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Technology stack</strong> — React, TypeScript and
            JavaScript, React Native, Node.js, PHP, AWS and Playwright
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What we build with Yembo's core team</h2>

        <h3>A 3D home editor for insurance claims</h3>
        <p>
          With Yembo's{" "}
          <a href="https://yembo.ai/insurance/claims">ScanMyHome®</a>,
          policyholders, inspectors and adjusters scan a home on their phone,
          and Yembo's AI 3D pipeline turns the scan into a 3D model of the
          house. Our part starts there: together with Yembo's core team, we
          built the in-browser 3D editor for that model. Users can adjust rooms,
          walls, doors, windows, objects and the roof, with full undo and redo.
          It is built on <a href="https://www.babylonjs.com/">Babylon.js</a>,
          and we designed its architecture.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hard part was making editing fast. We designed the heuristic
          algorithms and interaction model behind it, including{" "}
          <strong>bump-outs</strong>, <strong>wall merging</strong> and{" "}
          <strong>vertex merging</strong>. When someone moves a wall or a
          corner, the editor works out what they intend: it pushes part of a
          wall out into a bump-out, merges walls that line up into one, and
          joins corners that meet. The whole floor plan updates in a single
          move, where a generic vector editor would need many separate edits.
          The result is a state-of-the-art editor that is easy to use.
        </p>
        <figure class="diagram-figure">
          <FloorPlanDiagram />
          <figcaption>
            An illustration of the idea: drag one wall, and the rooms on both
            sides, the connected wall and the door update together.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Live video calls</h3>
        <p>
          Real-time video calls built into Yembo's products, developed together
          with the core team.
        </p>

        <h3>AI in the product</h3>
        <p>
          We integrate AI solutions into Yembo's products — both models
          developed in-house by Yembo's US team and services from outside
          vendors — so that AI results reach users as working product features.
        </p>

        <h3>From prototype to production</h3>
        <p>
          Yembo's CEO designs innovative prototypes; we help turn them into
          production features that hold up under real use and scale.
        </p>

        <h2>Keeping a large codebase modern</h2>
        <p>
          A platform deployed in more than 40 countries has to keep moving
          without breaking. Staying on modern technology is part of our
          responsibility, not an occasional project:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Migrated a large codebase from JavaScript and Redux to TypeScript
              and TanStack Query
            </strong>{" "}
            — safer changes through static types, and simpler, more consistent
            data fetching and caching
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Continuous refactoring</strong> of the applications and
            modules we are responsible for, improving their architecture while
            features keep shipping
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Automated end-to-end tests with Playwright</strong>, so
            refactoring and new features don't break what already works
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>It pays off in the era of AI-assisted development.</strong> We
          have always kept the code easy for people to work with: static types,
          consistent patterns, clear module boundaries and automated tests. It
          turns out the same qualities make it easy to develop with AI. Types
          and tests give AI coding tools the context to make the right change,
          and give our engineers a fast way to verify it.
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <p>
          This is how we prefer to work with every client: as part of the team,
          owning the outcome rather than renting out developers. We bring
          full-stack engineering, architecture for complex features like live
          video and 3D, experience turning AI and prototypes into production
          features, and the discipline to keep a large codebase modern for the
          long term.
        </p>

        <LeadBlock title="Looking for a long-term engineering partner?">
          We work as one accountable team alongside yours — product design,
          development, QA, and delivery — and stay as long as the product does.
          Tell us about your product and we will propose a team, a delivery plan
          and a first milestone.
        </LeadBlock>
      </div>
    </article>
    <ContactBanner />
  </div>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
